increasing numbers of protesters are  transitioning from standoffs on the  streets  to military and weapons training in the  jungle fronted by some of the ethnic  armies that have been fighting myanmar's  security forces  for decades last month in the city of  bago north of yangon  protesters faced one of the deadliest  crackdowns by security forces  more than 80 people were killed this  activist from bago was there  he was shot while on the streets  protesting he does not want to be  identified  he said the security forces were using  rocket-propelled grenades  after that day he and nine other  activists wanted to do more  so they left to train with one of the  korean ethnic armies  i've seen the ethnic current army fight  with myanmar's army on social media  i saw the current groups fighting  together against the army  i'm grateful for everybody that supports  each other in the crisis right now  i want democracy to return to myanmar  and that fighting has intensified in the  korean-controlled areas since the coup  in february  sending civilians fleeing for their  lives into hiding  eighteen-year-old dog guy lore is ethnic  corrine  and she has witnessed the fighting  between the rebels and the myanmar army  since she was a girl she started  training after finishing high school  initially refusing to learn how to use  firearms  but that changed once she saw the  attacks on her people  i come out with that i want to help  people and i want to have freedom in our  lives  i don't want the jinta taking advantage  of the people that's the reason why the  burmese protesters are coming to our  camp to receive training  the majority bamar ethnic group  historically has not become involved  with the ethnic rebel armies  that's changing so what you have is the  ethnic palma and the ethnic minorities  coming together  to fight against a common enemy which is  a military that is running amok  and this extends also uh to the youth  uh the gen zed youth in in in places  like mandalay and yangon  now have common cause with the ethnic  youth whether it be the koran or the  kachin or the karenia the mon youth  even though the two groups have  different views and experiences in  fighting the army  at the end of the months-long training  they're drawn closer together in what  appears to be  a growing and more unified front against  the junta's forces  scott heidler al jazeera
 

